And I feel like I really understood a lot of things about the Deal or No Deal system.

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:22.300
But also, more importantly, I understood how the banker worked.

00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:28.160
Deal or No Deal is a game where you pick a suitcase from, like, a selection of, I think it's like 20 suitcases or whatever.

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:29.480
You just pick the suitcase.

00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:31.140
So there's no trivia, right?

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:32.560
You don't need to know anything or whatever.

00:16:32.620 --> 00:16:33.460
You're just picking numbers.

00:16:33.700 --> 00:16:35.300
And they say, okay, pick these numbers.

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:38.000
And they range from $1 to $1 million.

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:39.960
And first, you pick a suitcase.

00:16:40.060 --> 00:16:41.300
And that's your suitcase.

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:44.420
And you can either eliminate other suitcases.

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:46.520
And so you want to try to eliminate low numbers.

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:49.500
So this way, the banker is like, oh, shit, I think he has the million dollars.

00:16:49.900 --> 00:16:53.720
And the one thing I learned is that it doesn't really matter whether you picked the million dollar suitcase.

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:56.100
Because you have terrible odds of picking the million dollar suitcase.

00:16:56.100 --> 00:17:05.960
But you have a great odd of getting an amazing offer from the banker early on, provided you don't fuck up and pick all the big numbers right in the very first round.

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:10.440
You can get an immediate offer of, like, $25,000.

00:17:10.840 --> 00:17:12.720
Like, quit the game right now and I'll give you $25,000.

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:16.800
And there are some game shows where the max prize isn't even $25,000.

00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:18.900
So get me on deal or no deal.

00:17:19.120 --> 00:17:20.760
I'm taking the first deal right away.

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:22.120
I'm their worst nightmare.

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:23.940
I'm not going to have any fun on the show.

00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:26.020
Not interesting. But I'm going to audition.

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:27.340
Like, I want the million.

00:17:27.340 --> 00:17:28.580
I'll do anything for the million.

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:29.780
My family needs a million.

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:32.940
Like, I will make them believe that I am in it for the whole thing.

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:35.240
And then I will take the deal on the first round.

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:37.340
I think you're right.

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:38.540
That takes no skill.

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:47.260
All you need is a little bit of game theory and statistics and, you know, the opposite of greed, contentment.

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:49.220
And you win.

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:50.780
It's the perfect game show for me.

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:55.800
Unlike a game I remember, I think, like, early 2000s maybe.

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:56.880
Maybe mid-2000s.

00:17:56.920 --> 00:17:59.580
It was called Dog Eat Dog.

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:01.320
Do you remember that? No.

00:18:01.540 --> 00:18:03.000
It was a weird game show.

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:04.640
It probably got, like, one season or whatever.

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:11.560
But it was very physically challenging oriented with, like, a – it was almost, like, a little bit like Ninja Warrior.

00:18:11.700 --> 00:18:12.800
But it all happened at once.

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:15.060
And there was always this pool that you could fall into.

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:17.420
And it was, like, teams.

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:18.960
And I just – I hate all of that.

00:18:19.040 --> 00:18:20.180
I hate working with other people.

00:18:20.500 --> 00:18:26.800
I hate the physicality of it because it was all, like, climbing ropes and stuff.

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:29.200
Things that, like, you know, scared me in fourth grade.

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:33.160
And then there was also, you know, trivia rounds in between.

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:35.720
And so don't ask me trivia questions.

00:18:36.040 --> 00:18:37.280
That's why I ask you guys.

00:18:37.300 --> 00:18:39.060
That's why I put you guys on the spot for the questions.

00:18:40.580 --> 00:18:45.520
Now, was – did Fear Factor ever have any, like, physical challenges?

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:49.720
Or was it always just, like, they bet you wouldn't want to eat bull testicle?

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:52.940
Yeah. You know, I do recall other physical challenges.

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:57.100
Okay. Yeah. Because otherwise that'd be pretty – because they're not going to, like, get you sick.

00:18:57.100 --> 00:18:58.340
That's a liability issue.

00:18:58.380 --> 00:19:02.760
So if it's just eating bull testicle, I guess I would do that.

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:05.940
Yeah, if they were just making a bull testicle the whole time, they would just call it the

00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:07.000
bull testicle show, right?

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:08.200
Bull testicle show with Joe Rogan.

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:11.440
Yeah. Okay.

00:19:11.440 --> 00:19:12.380
Who's the better wheel?

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:15.900
Wheel of Fortune or the wheel on the price is right?

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:18.200
The one where, you know, you try not to go over a dollar?

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:19.500
Oh, you know what?

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:22.720
I think it was the wheel on the price is – okay.

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:28.680
The fact that it's, like, a vertical wheel where the – on the face is, like, on the

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:31.640
spine of the wheel, I just like that better.

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:36.480
That always felt like it would be much more fun to spin than the Wheel of Fortune wheel.

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:39.720
It has great beeps, too, as you go between things.

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:43.660
See, that's what I don't like about the Wheel of Fortune wheel is it has this sound of,

00:19:43.780 --> 00:19:48.780
like, an engine starting up and then, you know, there's no, like – there should be

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:53.660
by now it should be – they should be taking a note from casinos with the slot machines

00:19:53.660 --> 00:19:55.780
and everything, and it should be completely digital.

00:19:55.920 --> 00:19:57.880
It should be something that lights up.

00:19:58.100 --> 00:20:02.820
It should have, like, cool, like, okay, kids at home, put your phone up on the screen to

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:05.480
see the AR graphics on the Wheel of Fortune wheel, right?

00:20:05.580 --> 00:20:09.440
Like, it needs shit like that, whereas the price is right wheel already understood the

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:14.880
assignment and it's very colorful colors and there's the cool beeps and the idea of not

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:16.200
going over a certain amount.

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:20.060
In Wheel of Fortune, it doesn't matter where you land, really, that's just the result.

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:27.060
In Price is Right, you actually get the opportunity to spin again and risk going over a dollar.

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:33.300
Ruh-roh. So I would actually prefer at this point, now that we're on our third iteration of Wheel

00:20:33.300 --> 00:20:38.600
of Fortune for the NES, I would have preferred an entire, you know, NES game where it's just

00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:43.780
spinning the wheel on the Price is Right and trying not to go over a dollar than ever having

00:20:43.780 --> 00:20:45.180
to play a third Wheel of Fortune.

00:20:45.180 --> 00:20:51.720
And I think that's basically how you feel in general, like, about just these games.

00:20:52.180 --> 00:20:53.900
Because, again, like...

00:20:53.900 --> 00:20:59.220
That's a great question because I was going to ask you, you know, have your thoughts on

00:20:59.220 --> 00:21:02.980
NES Wheel of Fortune changed at all since August 5th of 2022?

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:08.820
Because that was, you know, basically a year ago was the last time we played Wheel of Fortune.

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:13.700
And remember, we first reviewed Wheel of Fortune on May 22nd, 2020.

00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:18.300
So it has been three years in the making for us to change our opinion.

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:23.900
And I don't really have, you know, any different opinions than what I had in the first episode.

00:21:23.900 --> 00:21:27.820
No, it's basically, like, again, it's fine.

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:29.440
It's a fine...

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:31.680
Like, this is...

00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:34.180
These are the things that happen when you watch Wheel of Fortune.

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:36.320
But the fact that they just...

00:21:36.320 --> 00:21:41.780
Like, you pick a picture of the thing that you want and then it just says that you got that.

00:21:42.160 --> 00:21:44.040
That just feels so insulting.

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:49.340
I know that there really isn't much that you can do, but that...

00:21:49.340 --> 00:21:52.500
The whole prize thing is what really turns me off to these games.

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:57.320
If there's no actual intrinsic prize, I do not care.

00:21:57.740 --> 00:22:00.360
Absolutely. Final question here.

00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:03.140
Actually, it's a two-parter, so let's start with the first part.

00:22:03.580 --> 00:22:09.260
Is Wheel of Fortune Family Edition a remake, a re-release, a remaster, or something else?

00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:14.540
Yes. So, I'd say that this is just a version.

00:22:14.840 --> 00:22:16.580
Like, this is a release.

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:19.260
It's not a re-anything.

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:21.080
It's a release.

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:22.980
This is a game. Sorry, that is a re-something.

00:22:23.180 --> 00:22:26.220
No, you're right. It's sort of like...

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:28.080
It's not even blue and red.

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:35.320
Like, blue and red Pokemon are the same game with a different roster, basically.

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:40.900
And this is the same game with different letters and words.

00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:43.660
So, yeah. It's the same exact game.

00:22:43.820 --> 00:22:45.900
I think even the prizes are the same.

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:52.920
Yeah. Would they have done any better just releasing Wheel of Fortune Red and Wheel of Fortune Blue

00:22:52.920 --> 00:22:56.420
instead of these three different versions spaced out across a couple different years?

00:22:56.420 --> 00:23:03.920
I think it would be more clear because nothing in this tells me that it's Family Edition.

00:23:04.380 --> 00:23:06.900
Nothing in the other one told me it was whatever that was.

00:23:07.100 --> 00:23:07.920
Junior. Whatever you said it was.

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:10.740
Junior. Maybe they were simpler, but...

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:14.200
I mean, how often are you going to be able to compare, you know?

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:15.660
So, yeah.

00:23:15.780 --> 00:23:21.140
Just call them Edition 1, Edition 2, Edition 3.

00:23:21.880 --> 00:23:28.460
And so, with this whole Wheel of Fortune, you know, multiple releases, thought process that

00:23:28.460 --> 00:23:31.180
we're going on here, have you ever bought a game twice?

00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:36.880
Was there a promise of a dramatically different experience for a reason to buy it the second

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:39.880
time? Or was it just, well, now I need it on this platform?

00:23:40.720 --> 00:23:44.320
It's... With older games, it's a little bit of both.

00:23:44.520 --> 00:23:45.980
I just want it on this platform.

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:49.220
And I lost it, like, physically.

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:53.800
But other than that, I don't think there is a reason.

00:23:54.380 --> 00:23:55.840
Unless you're like...

00:23:55.840 --> 00:24:02.140
Again, like, maybe you have the OG Standard Edition, but now you want the one that comes

00:24:02.140 --> 00:24:10.060
with a card deck and other Fallout New Vegas things that you have that I don't, that I

00:24:10.060 --> 00:24:14.420
want. So, maybe that's the only other reason I'd buy a game again, you know?

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:19.980
Yep, yep. I was thinking just because, you know, Nintendo's been using this strategy for a little bit

00:24:19.980 --> 00:24:26.460
now with the Wii U games going on Switch, and they did, for Super Mario 3D World, they added

00:24:26.460 --> 00:24:29.880
in a new Bowser's Fury mode inside of it.

00:24:30.020 --> 00:24:33.280
So, it's like, technically, that's a whole other game, right?

00:24:33.880 --> 00:24:36.800
But you have to buy Super Mario 3D World.

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:38.580
And I was actually one of those guys who didn't.

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:43.860
I played many, many hours on the Wii U version and just didn't think it was worth $60 to get

00:24:43.860 --> 00:24:50.280
this cool little Bowser's Fury five-hour game in addition to a great game, Super Mario 3D

00:24:50.280 --> 00:24:51.580
World, but I didn't need it again.

00:24:51.620 --> 00:24:53.240
So, I didn't buy that game twice.

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:58.420
But there was promise of a dramatically different experience, just like how Wheel of Fortune

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:01.200
Family Edition is proposing all new puzzles, right?

00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:05.240
Like, they're saying, like, I know it's crazy, but I'm making that mental leap here.

00:25:05.480 --> 00:25:10.080
Wheel of Fortune Family Edition is promising something that is different than Wheel of Fortune

00:25:10.080 --> 00:25:11.300
the first time around.

00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:17.620
Yeah, but that's like, it's the loosest definition of content, you know?

00:25:18.120 --> 00:25:25.680
That's why they should just have a build a, you know, build a puzzle mode, and you can

00:25:25.680 --> 00:25:26.520
just do whatever you want.

00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:28.120
I'm sticking to that.

00:25:28.120 --> 00:25:34.800
And buying a game twice isn't something like buying Metal Gear Solid 3 and the new Metal

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:37.680
Gear Solid 3 Delta, if you choose to buy that.

00:25:37.840 --> 00:25:40.780
That's, you know, that falls into, well, it's a remake.

00:25:40.960 --> 00:25:41.980
That's a remake. Yeah.

00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:50.280
But I would say, even if it was an HD remaster or technically the final mix version of a game,

00:25:50.300 --> 00:25:53.380
I have bought many versions of Kingdom Hearts 2.

00:25:53.380 --> 00:25:55.020
So, I am...

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:57.560
Are they... Do they work the same way as, like, Persona?

00:25:57.680 --> 00:26:05.200
Because I've never played anything other than the first 10 hours of the regular Persona

00:26:05.200 --> 00:26:10.660
5, but I know that Persona 5 Deluxe Golden whatever, like, that's almost...

00:26:10.660 --> 00:26:12.480
Okay. That's even, like...

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:15.060
They've added so much that it could be called a different game.

00:26:15.180 --> 00:26:18.660
Is that sort of like how the Kingdom Hearts games work?

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:20.280
Theoretically, yes.

00:26:20.280 --> 00:26:23.240
So, you could have done that once in your life.

00:26:23.360 --> 00:26:26.980
There could have been a moment where you bought Kingdom Hearts 2 and you played that game and

00:26:26.980 --> 00:26:32.780
then you heard about this final mix and got that one and it had, you know, a lot of new

00:26:32.780 --> 00:26:37.940
boss fights and some additional story cut scenes and stuff like that.

00:26:38.100 --> 00:26:43.780
Not a completely different game, but still enough to warrant the, like, final mix edition.

00:26:44.020 --> 00:26:48.500
But then you also have to remember that they made an HD version of that on the PS3.

00:26:48.500 --> 00:26:52.660
And then an HD version of that on the PS4 and you could buy it again on Switch if you like.

00:26:52.820 --> 00:26:58.280
It's like, they just keep finding ways to re-release Kingdom Hearts 2 and I keep finding excuses to buy it.

00:27:00.900 --> 00:27:02.900
So, I am guilty of buying that game twice.

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:05.580
I think it is a lot different, though, than the Wheel of Fortune thing.

00:27:05.660 --> 00:27:10.460
I, of course, being the host, like to stretch the premises of these questions as far as possible.

00:27:10.840 --> 00:27:12.300
And I appreciate that about you.

00:27:12.300 --> 00:27:17.760
Now, speaking of stretching the premise of this whole show as far as possible, believe it or not,

00:27:17.980 --> 00:27:24.660
even though we have barely talked about the game, we do need to do the thing that we do every single week, Sean.

00:27:24.860 --> 00:27:29.460
And that is vote for Wheel of Fortune Family Edition on the Essential Games list.

00:27:32.440 --> 00:27:34.120
Wheel of Fortune Family Edition.

00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:38.860
I'm sure you know it by now, but it's not essential because they haven't changed anything.

00:27:38.860 --> 00:27:40.280
This is a problem.

00:27:40.580 --> 00:27:44.020
You have to at least change your game enough.

00:27:44.200 --> 00:27:45.660
The puzzles are not enough.

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:50.360
The puzzles are basically, you know, ROM hacks at this point.

00:27:50.700 --> 00:27:54.440
Other people could have changed the puzzles, you know, nowadays.

00:27:54.620 --> 00:27:57.840
Like, I'm surprised there aren't 30 ROM hacks of Wheel of Fortune.

00:27:57.920 --> 00:28:01.660
You know why that is? Because nobody cares that the puzzles are being changed.

00:28:01.660 --> 00:28:05.080
It's not enough to warrant the work that goes into this.

00:28:05.080 --> 00:28:10.860
And I would be shocked to find out that people owned more than one version of Wheel of Fortune that weren't collectors.

00:28:11.100 --> 00:28:16.220
Like, I seriously doubt a family had so much fun with Wheel of Fortune and then was in Toys R Us.

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:18.360
And the kid's like, mom, mom, family edition.

00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:19.380
We can all play together.

00:28:19.520 --> 00:28:23.740
Like, if that worked, kudos to Game Tech for figuring out that market.

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:25.660
But I highly doubt it existed.

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:27.820
I think you bought one of these and you called it a day.

00:28:27.820 --> 00:28:36.020
And we're calling it a day on Wheel of Fortune, not only for this episode, for this vote, but also for nostalgia as a whole.

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:38.500
Like, this is the last time we hopefully talk about Wheel of Fortune.

00:28:38.940 --> 00:28:42.300
Sean? Yeah, that had a lot of finality.

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:47.680
So I don't want to stretch this beyond that because you said it.

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:49.720
This game doesn't need to exist.

00:28:50.040 --> 00:28:51.660
Not essential. All right.

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:57.040
Next week we are playing, you know, the game show, Win, Lose, or Draw.

00:28:57.620 --> 00:28:58.780
What's going on right now, man?

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:02.560
Game show. That just sounds like a state.

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:06.620
Like, that's just describing, like, end states of a game, not a game.

00:29:06.900 --> 00:29:09.700
Yeah, well, theoretically that's the play on words, right?

00:29:09.700 --> 00:29:11.000
But I have some thoughts on that.

00:29:11.140 --> 00:29:12.720
I'll save them for the actual episode.

00:29:12.940 --> 00:29:18.260
But, yeah, Win, Lose, or Draw was also an actual game show, not just a video game game show.

00:29:18.940 --> 00:29:26.220
And if you're tired of game shows, don't worry, they're going to end because the week after that, oh, wait, it's Double Dare.

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:27.880
What the heck is going on?

00:29:28.060 --> 00:29:31.900
Did everybody figure out this is a great time to release game shows on the NES?

00:29:32.180 --> 00:29:35.800
Is this just a quirk of, like, the historic list?

00:29:36.080 --> 00:29:38.120
Like, this is very strange.

00:29:38.120 --> 00:29:47.100
The quirk there is that, obviously, Wheel of Fortune, Win, Lose, or Draw, and, you know, those are Ws.

00:29:47.240 --> 00:29:54.100
Yeah, those are Ws, though, so they end the month, obviously, when things don't have an actual day attached to them, just a release date.

00:29:54.200 --> 00:29:57.020
So these both released in March of 1990.

00:29:57.500 --> 00:30:04.980
But the crazy thing is that in April of 1990 there were many games that released, and Double Dare is the first alphabetically.

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:09.740
So it just looks like they all released, but it's still weird that they released all around the same time.

00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:12.540
Yeah, this is, I'm unhappy.

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:13.480
But I can't wait.

00:30:13.620 --> 00:30:17.560
I also can't wait to play more game shows with you.

00:30:17.700 --> 00:30:23.620
Yes, absolutely. And I'm sure we'll think of creative things for our Win, Lose, or Draw episode and our Double Dare episode,

00:30:23.620 --> 00:30:26.880
because a few of us are very familiar with Double Dare.

00:30:26.880 --> 00:30:32.940
And if that wasn't enough nostalgia for you, as I mentioned at the top of the episode,

00:30:33.080 --> 00:30:39.680
you can go to patreon.com slash nostalgia, and for $5, you can become a Nostalgia Bite-sized patron,

00:30:39.680 --> 00:30:42.800
and that gets you access to Nostalgia Bites, another show.

00:30:42.960 --> 00:30:44.760
We just finished our 20th episode.

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:47.520
It was on the original, the very first Fire Emblem.

00:30:47.520 --> 00:30:48.460
That's pretty cool.

00:30:48.620 --> 00:30:57.500
But we also have done Nuts and Milk, Devil World, Exerion, Etartic Adventure, Dig Dug, Starluster, Chaos World,

00:30:57.820 --> 00:30:59.880
Bio-Miracle Bokute Upa.

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:01.900
You know that you want to check out these games.

00:31:02.240 --> 00:31:09.080
And the next game on our list is The Glory of Heracles 2, Titan's Downfall.

00:31:09.100 --> 00:31:11.520
And that is a sequel to a game that we didn't cover.

00:31:11.520 --> 00:31:16.620
However, funny, a lot of people ask that question of like, do you, not people to me specifically,

00:31:16.880 --> 00:31:24.860
but people always ask like, do you need to play the game in order to understand the sequel or whatever?

00:31:24.940 --> 00:31:26.540
And how many times is that actually true?

00:31:27.160 --> 00:31:29.100
Like a game that we would play?

00:31:30.460 --> 00:31:31.600
Or any game, yeah.

00:31:31.780 --> 00:31:35.540
Like, just like, you know, oh, do I have to play the first one to understand this?

00:31:35.640 --> 00:31:36.560
Like, I feel like...

00:31:36.560 --> 00:31:39.020
I'd say it's not uncommon.

00:31:39.700 --> 00:31:41.700
Interesting. I feel like it's less common.

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:42.780
Like, it doesn't really matter.

00:31:42.980 --> 00:31:49.860
I think if it's a very, like, narrative-focused game, it's a linear narrative-focused game,

00:31:49.940 --> 00:31:53.060
then yeah, you probably should play the first couple.

00:31:53.360 --> 00:32:01.160
But if it's more so like a narrative game that just takes place in a world like a Dungeons & Dragons game,

00:32:01.160 --> 00:32:09.300
or if it's a game that's maybe released on an 8-bit system where storytelling isn't really at a point where it matters,

00:32:09.940 --> 00:32:11.620
then no, you don't need to play the first one.

00:32:11.620 --> 00:32:13.120
Yeah, sorry. I guess that was my point.

00:32:13.380 --> 00:32:21.300
Like, in the context of the show, you know, like, how many times is it like, you can't play Castlevania 2 because you didn't beat Castlevania 1?

00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:23.420
Like, there is a story, theoretically, right?

00:32:23.520 --> 00:32:27.120
Like, there are different Belmonts and stuff like that, but no one cares if you beat...

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:38.360
Well, I want to see if there's much, like, how much canon and lore has carried over when we eventually and soon play Snake's Revenge.

00:32:38.360 --> 00:32:39.960
So, I'm looking forward to that.

00:32:40.120 --> 00:32:41.180
All right. Sounds good.

00:32:41.480 --> 00:32:43.360
Look forward to many more episodes of Nostalgia.

00:32:43.360 --> 00:32:48.980
Remember, you can always write to us on the website, www.nostalgiacast.com.

00:32:49.080 --> 00:32:50.180
There's a little contact form there.

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:54.560
It'll send me an email. That'd be the best way to contact us because we used to say you could find us on Twitter.

00:32:54.760 --> 00:32:58.780
Now that thing's called X, and now it's collecting, like, data on people.

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:01.720
So, we'll see how longer we stay on there.

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:02.880
But technically, we're still on there for now.

00:33:02.960 --> 00:33:03.920
I'm just not checking it.

00:33:04.060 --> 00:33:09.200
So, if you're tweeting us there or X-ing us there, I'm sure a thousand people have made that joke,

00:33:09.900 --> 00:33:12.640
just know that there's other ways to get in contact with us.

00:33:12.860 --> 00:33:13.940
I'm Michael Esposito.

00:33:14.140 --> 00:33:17.320
You can find me up on that X platform.

00:33:17.320 --> 00:33:20.620
Not anymore. That's the last time I'm going to say that joke.

00:33:20.900 --> 00:33:22.000
Why don't we just leave?

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:47.440
I'm Michael Esposito.
